[{:db/id -1
 :adr/id "adr-2608260200-kotoba-lang-external-dependency-substitution"
 :adr/title "Every external dependency in kotoba-lang gets a decided destination, and a detector that checks it"
 :adr/status "accepted"
 :adr/date "2026-08-26"
 :adr/related ["adr-2607183300-kotoba-wasm-runtime-jvm-exit" "adr-2608136000-detectors-that-cannot-measure" "adr-2608260900-frontend-stack-retirement" "adr-2607173000-nbb-only-script-host"]
 :adr/body "## Context\n\nCLAUDE.md fixes the runtime order as `kotoba wasm` > `clojurewasm` >\nClojureScript > nbb and demotes the JVM and babashka to last resort.\n`90-docs/migration/kotoba-wasm-runtime-cutover.edn` goes further and declares\n`:new-jvm-runtime-dependencies :forbidden`. Neither document says what the\ncurrent external surface actually is, so the question an author faces at the\nmoment it matters — *may I depend on this?* — had no answer that could be\nlooked up. It could only be re-derived, and it was re-derived differently\neach time.\n\n`verify-jvm-dependency-surface` answers a nearby but different question:\nWHERE a JVM is still required. That is a census of hosts. What was missing was\na census of coordinates, with a decided destination for each.\n\n## What was measured\n\n2,129 of 2,143 west-registered `orgs/kotoba-lang/` paths, on 2026-08-26. The\nwhole external surface is **26 distinct JVM runtime coordinates and 110 npm\nruntime packages** — 191 counting dev-only tooling. That is small enough to\ndecide once, per coordinate, and then check.\n\nThe measurement reshaped the work. Most of the surface is *already*\nreplaceable:\n\n- The foundational stdlib (ADR-kotoba-lang-foundational-stdlib) ships\n  `coll` `spec` `json` `wit` `async` `time` `fs` `http` `io` `test` `fmt`\n  `lsp` `scheduler` `store` `lint`, plus `net` `crypto` `tls` `kv`.\n- The origin-plane mirrors ship `org-nist-sha2` `org-ietf-blake2`\n  `org-ietf-ed25519` `org-ietf-cbor` `io-multiformats` `io-ipld-car`\n  `org-ietf-deflate` `org-ietf-x509` `org-ietf-asn1` `org-postgresql-wire`.\n- `eth-crypto` already implements secp256k1 point arithmetic and RFC 6979\n  deterministic ECDSA in portable `.cljc`, which covers most of what\n  `@noble/curves` is used for outside ed25519.\n\nSo the request *design and implement every external dependency as a library*\nresolves, for the large majority by usage, into **rewiring an import** rather\nthan writing a library. Recording that is the main value of this decision:\nwithout it, the same libraries get proposed again.\n\nFour claimed replacements did NOT survive checking, and saying so is the\npoint of measuring rather than assuming. `com-opc-ua`, `com-quic-http3` and\n`com-modbus-tcp` read like protocol implementations from their names; each is\na ~190-line CRUD scaffold over a schema. Naming them as the destination for\n`org.eclipse.milo`, `tech.kwik/kwik` and `com.digitalpetri.modbus` would have\nbeen false.\n\n## Decision\n\n**1. `manifest/dependency-substitution.edn` is the source of truth for what\nevery external dependency is supposed to become.** Each entry carries a\n`:disposition`:\n\n| disposition | meaning |\n|---|---|\n| `:structural` | the host language, its compiler, or a render backend. Not a library anyone here can write; the exit is the `.kotoba` migration |\n| `:first-party-exists` | a replacement is shipped; the work is switching the import |\n| `:gap-closed` | there was no first-party equivalent and one was written |\n| `:gap-open` | no first-party equivalent and none written; names what it would be called |\n| `:frozen-legacy` | covered by an existing cutover contract with its own exit evidence |\n| `:tooling` | dev-time only; never resolved by shipped code |\n\nAn entry may name one `:coordinate`, several under `:coordinates`, or a name\nprefix under `:pattern`. One `@radix-ui/` rule beats twenty-seven identical\nentries, and a family that grows does not silently fall out of the ledger.\nThe longest matching prefix wins, so a specific rule can still override its\nfamily.\n\n**2. `scripts/verify-dependency-substitution.cljs` enforces it.** Four\nfindings:\n\n- `unlisted-external-dependency` — a coordinate resolved by shipped code that\n  the ledger does not name. This is the finding the ledger exists to produce:\n  a new dependency that arrives without a decision.\n- `substitution-target-missing` — the ledger names a `:target` that west does\n  not register. A ledger pointing at a library nobody wrote is worse than no\n  ledger, because it reads as though the migration were already possible.\n- `builtin-shadowing-dependency` — see below.\n- `substitution-available` — replaceable today, still imported. Reported at\n  `:info`, because it is a plan and not a defect.\n\nIt refuses to report a pass it did not measure: fewer than 500 of the\nregistered paths on disk prints `SCANNED\\t0` and exits **2** — neither 0 nor\n1 — because *I could not look* and *I looked and it was clean* must not be the\nsame value (ADR-2608136000). Demonstrated: run in a worktree with no `orgs/`\ncheckouts, it exits 2 rather than reporting a clean tree.\n\n**3. Two libraries were written for gaps that had no first-party equivalent.**\n\n- **`kotoba-lang/org-ietf-websocket`** — RFC 6455 framing and handshake,\n  portable `.cljc`, zero dependencies. `inga-node` and `torihiki-node`\n  depended on npm `ws` for what is arithmetic over bytes; `ws` is a Node addon\n  boundary, so the framing vanished on any JVM or kotoba-WASM target. 20\n  tests, 117 assertions, green on both runtimes. Test vectors are RFC 6455\n  §5.7 verbatim.\n- **`kotoba-lang/org-msgpack`** — MessagePack, portable `.cljc`, zero\n  dependencies. The format already existed twice and neither copy could be\n  shared: `checkpointer` carries a JVM-only codec with throwing ClojureScript\n  stubs, and the same repository's JavaScript side depends on\n  `@msgpack/msgpack`. 16 tests, 214 assertions, green on both runtimes, with\n  20 known answers taken from the real `@msgpack/msgpack` via checkpointer's\n  own generator.\n\n## What the two-runtime rule caught\n\nBoth libraries ship a JVM runner and a ClojureScript runner, and that is not\na formality. Four defects were found that one runtime reported as green:\n\n1. **ClojureScript takes a bit-shift count mod 32.** A 64 KiB WebSocket frame\n   whose length was encoded with `>>> 48` came back as `>>> 16` and announced\n   `0x0001000000010000`. The JVM computed it correctly throughout.\n   Reintroducing the bug today still leaves `clojure -M:test` green and turns\n   the ClojureScript run red; that asymmetry was measured, not asserted.\n2. **`(map int s)` on a string.** Correct on the JVM; under ClojureScript a\n   character is a one-character string and `int` of one is not a code point,\n   so `Sec-WebSocket-Accept` was a digest of zeros.\n3. **A guard placed after the fold it was guarding.** The 64-bit length checks\n   ran after eight bytes were folded into a number — where the JVM throws\n   `long overflow` first. The guard was unreachable in precisely the case it\n   existed for. Same defect, independently, in msgpack's `read-int`, which\n   folded unsigned and subtracted `2^(8n)` afterwards.\n4. **Encoding a negative int64 as `n + 2^64`.** Overflows a JVM long before\n   the value is written. Now floored `mod`, which returns the two's-complement\n   word on both runtimes.\n\nDefect 3 is the class CLAUDE.md names in ADR-2608136000 — a check that cannot\nrun returning the same value as a check that ran and passed — appearing twice\nin one afternoon, in code written the same afternoon by someone who had just\nread that rule.\n\n## What the census found on the side\n\n`app-scap` declares `crypto`, `fs`, `net`, `path`, `tls` and `workflow` as npm\n`dependencies`, at `latest`. **These do not resolve to the Node builtins.** npm\nserves a real, unrelated package for each name: `fs@0.0.1-security` is npm's\nreclaimed-squat placeholder, `tls@0.0.1` is a stub, and `crypto`, `net` and\n`path` are abandoned userland shims. A range of `latest` means whatever is\npublished at install time. This buys nothing — `require(\"fs\")` reaches the\nbuiltin regardless — and adds six supply-chain edges. It is now its own\ndetector finding rather than a line in a table, because it is a class that\nrecurs and is invisible by inspection.\n\n`murakumo` declares `curves@^1.7.0`. The npm package `curves` is at 1.1.0 and\nunrelated, so that range **cannot resolve at all**. It is a typo for\n`@noble/curves` that has never been installed. Fixing it means deleting the\nline.\n\n## Consequences\n\n- The question *may I depend on this?* now has a file to look in, and a new\n  dependency that arrives without a decision becomes a finding on the next\n  detector tick instead of arriving silently.\n- The remaining `:gap-open` set is small and named: Argon2 (RFC 9106),\n  BLS12-381, SQLite's file format, QUIC, OPC UA, Modbus framing, the CDP wire,\n  HPKE (RFC 9180), and TOML. Each entry says what it would be called and why\n  it has not been done. Two of them — Argon2 on top of the shipped\n  `org-ietf-blake2`, and Modbus, whose MBAP header plus PDU is small — are\n  the tractable next ones.\n- `org.xerial/sqlite-jdbc` is listed as `:gap-open` but flagged as worth\n  questioning before building. `kotobase-engine-lsm` is the first-party\n  engine; a SQLite reader adds only compatibility with files other tools\n  wrote, which may not be worth a file-format implementation.\n- Usage counts are deliberately NOT written into the ledger. They were\n  measured to decide the dispositions and they are here, in a document that\n  carries a date. A count written into a policy file becomes a constant that\n  gets quoted after it stops being true — the failure CLAUDE.md documents\n  about its own fleet-ci section. Re-measure with `--edn`.\n\n## Status of the enforcement\n\nFirst run against the real checkout, 2026-08-26: 2,131 of 2,145 registered\npaths present, 191 distinct coordinates, **0 unlisted**, **0 missing\ntargets**, 5 builtin-shadowing dependencies, 99 coordinates replaceable\ntoday. Both failure modes were shown to discriminate: pointing a `:target` at\na repository nobody wrote turns it red, and removing a coordinate the\nworkspace actually uses turns it red. Restoring returns it to the standing\nstate."}]
